    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d206">

                <pb facs="0362" n="336" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v16/pg336"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">396.1 GE/6–154: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Korea</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Briggs</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>niact</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-06-01T00:00:00+08:30">June 1,
                        1954—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1278. Repeated information Tokyo 758, niact Geneva 144. Re Geneva’s 107 (Dulte
                    137 to Department).<anchor n="1" xml:id="d206-f1"/> In existing circumstances I
                    agree that anything short of “complete and unconditional termination” at Geneva
                    will probably create major problem with ROK. That, however, has been inherent in
                    Rhee’s attitude ever since last year when we insisted on armistice, against his
                    passionately held conviction that stalemate on battlefield would not lead to
                    unification of his country within predictable time. All Rhee’s views—toward POW
                    exchange, toward neutrals and neutralism, toward Communist advance into
                    Southeast Asia, toward fall of Dien Bien Phu, and now toward his exclusion (as
                    he sees it) from forthcoming Washington military talks<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d206-f2"/>—derive in substantial measure from Rhee’s hatred of
                    armistice and his belief that whatever develops from deadlock created by
                    armistice, time is running out.</p>
                <p>If we ever had any doubts on that score, four weeks of almost daily negotiations
                    with Rhee while Ambassador Dean was here ought to have dissipated them.</p>
                <p>As Rhee views it from Seoul, clean break at Geneva with no leftovers and no cold
                    salad gathering mould in UN icebox is least unproductive result likely to follow
                    conference deliberations. Moreover, anything that Rhee interprets as temporizing
                    or appeasement will multiply possibility he may publicly denounce Geneva
                    thereafter withdrawing ROK delegation.</p>
                <p>Foregoing is not intended to imply that clean break at Geneva would solve problem
                    of ROK post conference behavior. Fuzzy ambiguous <pb facs="0363" n="337"
                        xml:id="pg337"/> break would, however, make Rhee more difficult to deal with
                    in immediate future than clean one.</p>
                <p>Deptel 968 (to Tokyo 2656, to Geneva Tosec 316)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d206-f3"/>
                    just received. I concur with views expressed therein especially introductory
                    paragraphs and numbered paragraphs 1, 3 and 4.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Briggs</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
